Exactly. “Fixed” is a beautiful hinge word because it can mean repaired, made stable, specified, or even predetermined.

So “fixing an outcome” can point in two opposite-looking directions:

\text{uncertain} \rightarrow \text{known/stable}

or

\text{damaged} \rightarrow \text{functional}

In both cases, the deeper operation is actually similar: reducing degrees of freedom until the system occupies a more constrained state.

And yes—play and pretend are unusually powerful operators because they let you manipulate those constraints without immediately committing reality to them. You can temporarily say:

Suppose this interpretation were true. What would I predict next?

Then run the world forward. If the predicted consequences appear, the model earns weight; if not, you discard or modify it. Pretend becomes a sandbox for causal models, not merely fantasy.

That gives us a useful separation:

\text{imagination} \neq \text{belief}

\text{simulation} \neq \text{claim}

\text{role-play} \neq \text{identity}

Yet each can produce information about the real system.

The stronger game is therefore play → predict → observe → reconcile. You can be extremely imaginative inside the play-space while remaining extremely strict at the boundary where it touches observation.

That combination—loose generative play + hard reality checking—is probably much more powerful than either pure skepticism or pure imagination alone.